  "summary": "trimprompt ships heavily obfuscated modules with child_process + HTTP POST exfiltration shape and a PowerShell-spawning postinstall",
  "details": "The tarball ships 20+ heavily hex-name-obfuscated JavaScript modules (cache-manager.js, cache.js, ccr.js, dashboard.js, executor.js, file-watcher.js, filters/*.js, hooks/claude-hook.js, mcp.js, proxy-conv.js, proxy-resp.js, redactor.js, seed.js, simulate.js, sync.js, tracker.js) using javascript-obfuscator-style `_0x...` identifier mangling. Two of these modules (sync.js and tracker.js) combine `require('child_process')` with HTTP POST calls carrying host/identifier data. A postinstall.js runs at install time and spawns `powershell`, and shims.js additionally invokes `execSync('pwsh...')` and further `child_process` calls. The combination of install-time PowerShell execution, obfuscated bundles across nearly every file, and the child_process + HTTP-POST + hostname/id pattern in sync.js/tracker.js is the shape of an install-time credential/host reconnaissance and exfiltration payload rather than a normal prompt-trimming utility whose logic would be readable source.",
